      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Since the lines intersect at $(6, 10)$, we can substitute these values into the equations to find $m$ and $b$.\n\nFor the line $y = mx + 4$:\n\n\\[\n10 = 6m + 4\n\\]\n\nSolving for $m$:\n\n\\[\n10 - 4 = 6m\n\\]\n\n\\[\n6 = 6m\n\\]\n\n\\[\nm = 1\n\\]\n\nNow, we know $m = 1$.\n\nFor the line $y = 3x + b$:\n\n\\[\n10 = 3(6) + b\n\\]\n\nSolving for $b$:\n\n\\[\n10 = 18 + b\n\\]\n\n\\[\n10 - 18 = b\n\\]\n\n\\[\nb = -8\n\\]\n\nNow, we have $m = 1$ and $b = -8$.\n\nTo find $b + m$:\n\n\\[\nb + m = -8 + 1 = -7\n\\]\n\nSo the value of $b + m$ is $\\boxed{-7}$. Let's verify this step-by-step using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given point of intersection\nx_point = 6\ny_point = 10\n\n# Line equations\n# y = mx + 4\n# y = 3x + b\n\n# Using the point (6, 10) to find m\nm = (y_point - 4) / x_point\n\n# Using the point (6, 10) to find b\nb = y_point - 3 * x_point\n\n# Calculate b + m\nb_plus_m = b + m\nprint(b_plus_m)\n```",
